+### Remembered consent grants (story persistent-consent-grants)
+
+Nebu remembers a user's "Allow" for an OAuth client (REQ-2026-003, migration 000057, table `oauth_consent_grants`): the next sign-in with the same client skips the consent screen and the callback issues the authorization code itself. This relaxes FR1 of ADR-033 ("consent on every authorization"); see the addendum there.
+
+- **What is remembered.** One grant per user and client: the normalised scope (sorted unique tokens, without the per-login `<prefix>device:<id>` token: the device id is not part of the remembered scope, matrix-js-sdk and FluffyChat use a new one for every login), the exact redirect URI the user allowed and `client_approved_at`, the `approved_at` of the client registration at that time (the identity binding). Nothing per login is stored (no code, state, nonce, challenge, device or token). An Allow replaces scope and redirect URI with what was just allowed (a narrower Allow narrows the grant); `created_at` is the first Allow, `granted_at` the latest.
+- **When the screen is skipped.** Only when the client is `approved`, a grant exists for this user, the redirect URI is exactly equal to the remembered one, the requested scope (after the existing narrowing: `profile`, `email` and other unknown tokens are ignored and never granted) is a subset of the remembered one, `client_approved_at` equals the client's current `approved_at`, and the request does not carry `prompt=consent` (`consent` among the space separated values; `prompt=login consent` asks). All of it is read in ONE statement that joins the client row, so a revocation that has committed can never be missed. A narrower request skips and gets only the narrower scope; a broader one (for example `openid` added) or another Matrix prefix asks again.
+- **What a skip does NOT change.** The sign-in at the identity provider still happens (a skip is not a login skip), the freshness verdict, gate l (a missing IdP refresh token), the device holder rule (another app's device is refused with `device_in_use`) and the PKCE challenge, `state`, `nonce`, device id and redirect URI binding of the code are exactly as after an Allow; the code row is the same. The skip applies whatever the freshness verdict is (it only drives a notice on the consent screen today). Rate limits are unchanged; the skip needs no consent row and sets no consent cookie.
+- **When a grant ends.** Revocation of the client (`Nebu.OAuth.ClientStore.revoke/3`) deletes the grants of every user in the same transaction (a failing audit or a failing delete rolls the revocation back). A client that is approved again later (only possible through the owner override of the revocation story) does not inherit a grant: its `approved_at` is new and a surviving row no longer matches. The user can withdraw a grant on the account page (`/account/apps`, "Apps"): own grants only, CSRF and the same browser-POST checks as the device sign-out, no fresh sign-in needed, and it does not end existing sessions or refresh tokens (signing a device out is the Devices page). GDPR erasure deletes the user's grants (`oauth_consent_grants` is in `UserDeletion.erased_tables/0`). A grant has no expiry: withdrawal, revocation and erasure end it.
+- **Fail towards asking.** If the grant table cannot be read, the consent screen is shown; if an Allow cannot be remembered (unreadable table) the code is still issued and one value-free warning is logged, the user is simply asked again next time. The revoke transaction does fail closed when its delete of the grants fails.
+- **Migration 000057** creates `oauth_consent_grants` (grants `SELECT, INSERT, DELETE` plus a column level `UPDATE (scope, redirect_uri, client_approved_at, granted_at)`; RLS enabled and forced) and adds `oauth_authz_requests.force_consent BOOLEAN NOT NULL DEFAULT false` (carries `prompt=consent` to the callback). Rolling deploy: a node of the previous release never reads a grant, it keeps asking on every login; grants written by new nodes are not used there. *Rollback:* the down migration drops the table and the column (every user is asked again); roll the code back together with it.
+- **Operator queries.** Who allowed which client: `SELECT user_id, client_id, scope, redirect_uri, granted_at FROM oauth_consent_grants ORDER BY granted_at DESC;`. Force everybody to be asked again for one client (without revoking it): `DELETE FROM oauth_consent_grants WHERE client_id = '<client id>';`.
+
